sections.body:
This is where the three forms genuinely diverge in behavior, not just style. **Function declarations are hoisted completely** -- including their entire body -- to the top of their enclosing scope, so you can call one from code that appears above its definition in the file:

```javascript
console.log(add(2, 3));   // 5 -- works, even though add is defined below

function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
```

**Function expressions and arrow functions do not get this treatment.** They're just a variable holding a function value, so they follow whatever hoisting rule applies to the variable declaration itself: a `var add = function...` is hoisted and pre-initialized to `undefined`, so calling it before its assignment line runs throws `TypeError: add is not a function` (you're calling undefined, not a function); a `const add = () => ...` sits in the temporal dead zone until its line runs, so calling it early throws `ReferenceError: Cannot access 'add' before initialization`. Either way, calling a function expression or arrow function before its definition line has executed fails -- the mechanism differs, but the practical rule is the same: only true function declarations can be safely called from above their own definition.

This is the single most important practical difference between the function forms, and it's the reason arrow functions spread through the language so fast once they arrived. A regular function's `this` is **not** determined by where the function is defined -- it's determined by **how it's called** (the call site), re-evaluated fresh on every call: call it as `obj.method()` and `this` is `obj`; call the exact same function as a bare `fn()` and `this` is `undefined` (in strict mode/modules) or the global object (in old-style non-strict code); call it with `.call()`, `.apply()`, or `.bind()` and `this` is whatever you explicitly pass.

This becomes a real bug the moment you extract a method and pass it around as a callback, because the call site changes and drags `this` along with it:

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Priya",
  greet: function() {
    console.log("Hi, " + this.name);
  }
};

user.greet();                 // "Hi, Priya" -- called as user.greet(), this is user
setTimeout(user.greet, 100);  // "Hi, undefined" -- called as a bare function, this isn't user anymore
```

**Arrow functions solve this by not having their own `this` at all.** An arrow function captures `this` lexically -- from its enclosing scope, at the point it's defined in the source, exactly like it captures any other closed-over variable -- and that captured `this` never changes no matter how or where the arrow function is later called. This is exactly why arrow functions became the default choice for callbacks and event handlers: a class method that needs `this` to stay pointed at the instance, even when handed off as a callback (a `setTimeout` callback, an event handler, a `.then()` callback), reliably keeps the right `this` if it's written as an arrow function or if the callback wrapping it is one. This was *the* defining pain point of React's class-component era -- methods had to be manually `.bind(this)`'d in the constructor, or written as arrow-function class properties, purely to survive being passed as `onClick={this.handleClick}` -- and even though function components and hooks have made manual binding far less common day to day, understanding this mechanism is still what makes `this` predictable anywhere you encounter it, in React code or otherwise.

examples.code:
console.log(declared(1, 2));   // 3 -- works fine

try {
  console.log(expressed(1, 2));  // throws before this line even finishes
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.message);        // "Cannot access 'expressed' before initialization"
}

function declared(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

const expressed = (a, b) => a + b;

const timer = {
  seconds: 0,
  start: function() {
    // BROKEN: a regular function passed to setInterval loses `this`
    setInterval(function() {
      this.seconds++;               // this is NOT timer here
      console.log(this.seconds);    // NaN, NaN, NaN...
    }, 1000);
  },
  startFixed: function() {
    // FIXED: arrow function captures `this` from start's own call-site
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds++;               // this is timer, inherited lexically
      console.log(this.seconds);    // 1, 2, 3...
    }, 1000);
  }
};

