sections.body:
Because arrays are reference types (see the Data Types concept), assigning one array variable to another does not copy the array -- it copies the reference, so both variables end up pointing at the exact same underlying array:

```javascript
const original = [1, 2, 3];
const alias = original;
alias.push(4);
console.log(original);   // [1, 2, 3, 4] -- changed, even though you only touched `alias`
```

This is frequently unintentional -- someone writes `const copy = original` meaning to make an independent copy, then mutates `copy` (via `push`, `splice`, direct index assignment, or any other mutating method) and is surprised the 'original' changed too. There was never a copy; there was only ever one array with two names. To make a genuine, independent (shallow) copy, use `original.slice()`, `[...original]` (spread, covered fully in a later concept), or `Array.from(original)` -- any of these create a new array object with the same elements, so mutating the copy leaves the original untouched.

examples.code:
const scores = [10, 1, 2, 21];
const result = scores.sort();

console.log(result);   // [1, 10, 2, 21] -- sorted as strings, not numbers
console.log(scores);   // [1, 10, 2, 21] -- the ORIGINAL array was mutated too
console.log(result === scores);  // true -- same array, two names

const fixed = [10, 1, 2, 21].sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(fixed);    // [1, 2, 10, 21] -- correct numeric order

const cart = ["apple", "bread"];

const alias = cart;
alias.push("milk");
console.log(cart);    // ["apple", "bread", "milk"] -- unintentionally changed

const realCopy = cart.slice();
realCopy.push("eggs");
console.log(cart);      // ["apple", "bread", "milk"] -- untouched this time
console.log(realCopy);  // ["apple", "bread", "milk", "eggs"]

