sections.body:
Exactly like arrays, plain objects are reference types: a variable holding an object holds a reference to it, not the object's data directly. Assigning `const b = a` for an object `a` copies that reference -- `b` and `a` become two names for the identical underlying object, not two independent objects:

```javascript
const a = { count: 1 };
const b = a;
b.count = 99;
console.log(a.count);   // 99 -- a and b were always the same object
```

This has a direct consequence for comparison too: `===` on two objects checks whether they're the *same reference*, not whether their contents look alike. `{ x: 1 } === { x: 1 }` is `false` -- two separately-created objects, even with identical-looking contents, are never `===` equal, because they're two different objects in memory. Only `a === b` where both names actually refer to the same object (as in the snippet above) is `true`.

A key inside an object literal can be computed from an expression by wrapping it in `[ ]` at the point you define the object, rather than only being settable afterward via bracket-notation assignment:

```javascript
const field = "score";
const result = { [field]: 100, [`${field}Max`]: 150 };
// equivalent to { score: 100, scoreMax: 150 }
```

This is especially useful when building an object dynamically -- for instance, constructing a lookup table keyed by some runtime value (a user ID, a category name) without needing a separate assignment statement after the literal is created.

examples.code:
const product = { name: "Widget", "unit-price": 9.99 };

console.log(product.name);           // "Widget" -- valid identifier, dot works
console.log(product["unit-price"]);  // 9.99 -- hyphen isn't valid in an identifier, dot fails here
// console.log(product.unit-price); // this would be parsed as (product.unit) - price -- NOT what you want

const key = "name";
console.log(product[key]);   // "Widget" -- key is a variable, so bracket notation is required

const settings = {
  theme: "dark",
  notifications: { email: true, sms: false }
};

const userSettings = { ...settings };
userSettings.theme = "light";                  // independent, top-level string
userSettings.notifications.sms = true;         // mutates the SHARED nested object

console.log(settings.theme);                    // "dark" -- unaffected
console.log(settings.notifications.sms);        // true  -- unintentionally changed!

// A real fix for the nested object:
const safeCopy = { ...settings, notifications: { ...settings.notifications } };

