sections.body:
Before promises existed, asynchronous operations in JavaScript were handled with **callbacks** -- functions passed in to be called later, once an operation finished. This works fine for one async step, but chaining several dependent async steps together meant nesting callback inside callback inside callback:

```javascript
getUser(userId, function(user) {
  getPosts(user.id, function(posts) {
    getComments(posts[0].id, function(comments) {
      console.log(comments);
      // ...and error handling has to be duplicated at every single level
    }, function(err) { console.error(err); });
  }, function(err) { console.error(err); });
}, function(err) { console.error(err); });
```

This pattern, nicknamed **'callback hell,'** has two real problems, not just an aesthetic one: the nesting grows one level deeper for every additional async step, making the code progressively harder to read top-to-bottom; and error handling has to be wired up separately at every single level, since there's no shared, uniform way for an error at any step to propagate up to one common handler. Promises, and later async/await, were built specifically to solve both problems at once.

`async`/`await` is syntax that lets you write promise-based code that reads like ordinary synchronous code, without changing what's actually happening underneath -- it's built entirely on top of the promise mechanism already described. Marking a function `async` does two things: inside its body, you can use `await somePromise` to pause that function's own execution at that line until the promise settles, then continue with the resolved value directly (no `.then()` callback needed); and -- this is a genuinely common surprise -- **an `async` function always returns a promise**, even if its body has a plain `return 5;` with no promise in sight. The returned value gets automatically wrapped in a fulfilled promise; to get the plain `5` back out, the caller still needs to `await` the call (from inside another async function) or `.then()` it, exactly as with any other promise.

```javascript
async function getValue() {
  return 5;
}

console.log(getValue());          // Promise {<fulfilled>: 5} -- NOT 5 directly
getValue().then(v => console.log(v));   // 5
```

`await` only pauses the `async` function it's inside -- it does not block the rest of the program. Other code (other functions, other event handlers, the rest of the call stack) keeps running normally while one async function is paused at an `await`; this is exactly why `await` never freezes the page the way, say, a long synchronous loop would. `try`/`catch` is the async/await equivalent of `.catch()`: wrapping an `await` in a `try` block catches that promise's rejection as a regular thrown error, letting you handle async errors with the same syntax you'd use for synchronous ones.

A genuinely common real-world performance bug: awaiting inside a loop over several **independent** async operations, which forces them to run one at a time even though nothing requires that:

```javascript
// SLOW: each fetch waits for the previous one to fully finish first
const results = [];
for (const id of userIds) {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);   // blocks the loop until THIS one resolves
  results.push(user);
}
```

Each iteration's `await` pauses the loop until that specific fetch resolves before even *starting* the next one -- so three independent one-second fetches take three full seconds in sequence, even though none of them depends on any of the others' results, and they could all be in flight over the network simultaneously. The fix is to start every operation first (without awaiting inside the loop), collecting the resulting promises, and then wait for all of them together with **`Promise.all`**:

```javascript
// FAST: all fetches start immediately, running concurrently
const promises = userIds.map(id => fetchUser(id));
const results = await Promise.all(promises);   // waits for all of them together
```

`userIds.map(fetchUser)` calls `fetchUser` for every id immediately, one after another with no waiting in between, so every request is already in flight before `Promise.all` is even reached; `Promise.all` then waits for every one of those promises to settle and resolves to an array of all their results, in the same order as the input. One caveat worth knowing: `Promise.all` **rejects as soon as any single promise in the group rejects**, discarding the results of the ones that had already succeeded -- if you need every outcome regardless of individual failures (some succeeded, some failed, and you want to know which), `Promise.allSettled` is the tool for that instead, since it always resolves with a status/value or status/reason for every promise, never rejecting itself.

examples.code:
// Callback style (the problem)
getUser(1, function(user) {
  getPosts(user.id, function(posts) {
    console.log(posts);
  }, handleError);
}, handleError);

// Promise chain
getUser(1)
  .then(user => getPosts(user.id))
  .then(posts => console.log(posts))
  .catch(handleError);

// async/await -- same logic, reads top-to-bottom like sync code
async function loadPosts() {
  try {
    const user = await getUser(1);
    const posts = await getPosts(user.id);
    console.log(posts);
  } catch (err) {
    handleError(err);
  }
}

async function double(x) {
  return x * 2;
}

const result = double(5);
console.log(result);        // Promise {<fulfilled>: 10} -- not 10

double(5).then(value => console.log(value));   // 10

async function useIt() {
  const value = await double(5);
  console.log(value);       // 10
}
useIt();

// Each fetchUser call is assumed to take about 1 second

// SLOW: ~3 seconds total -- each await blocks the next iteration from starting
async function loadSequential(ids) {
  const users = [];
  for (const id of ids) {
    users.push(await fetchUser(id));
  }
  return users;
}

// FAST: ~1 second total -- all three requests are in flight at once
async function loadConcurrent(ids) {
  const promises = ids.map(id => fetchUser(id));
  return Promise.all(promises);
}

