sections.body:
`fetch(url, options)` starts an HTTP request and returns a promise. That promise resolves to a `Response` object once the response has *started* arriving -- but the `Response` object is not the data itself; reading the actual body is a **separate step**, because the body can be large or streamed, and isn't eagerly parsed into memory just because the response headers have arrived. To get JSON data out, you call `response.json()`, which itself **also returns a promise** (parsing the body is itself an asynchronous step):

```javascript
const response = await fetch("/api/users/1");
const data = await response.json();
console.log(data);
```

That's two separate `await`s for two separate promises -- one for the response arriving, one for its body being read and parsed. Forgetting the second `await` (or the second `.then()`, in promise-chain style) is a common early mistake, leaving you with a still-pending promise where you expected data.

Wrapping an `await`ed `fetch` call in a `try`/`catch` handles both failure modes cleanly, as long as you remember to manually throw for the HTTP-error-status case, since `fetch` won't do it for you:

```javascript
async function getJson(url) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status}`);
    }
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("getJson failed:", error.message);
    throw error;   // re-throw so the caller can also react if it needs to
  }
}
```

A genuine network failure (no connectivity, DNS failure) makes the `await fetch(url)` line itself throw, landing directly in the `catch` block. A 404 or 500 response, by contrast, makes `await fetch(url)` resolve successfully -- it's the explicit `if (!response.ok) throw new Error(...)` line that turns that case into something the same `catch` block can also handle uniformly. Without that explicit check, an HTTP error response would silently fall through as if it had succeeded.

examples.code:
async function getUser(id) {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const user = await response.json();
  return user;
}

const user = await getUser(1);
console.log(user);   // { id: 1, name: "Amara", ... }

// This looks like it handles errors, but it doesn't catch HTTP error statuses:
fetch("/api/users/99999")   // a user id that doesn't exist -- server responds 404
  .then(res => res.json())
